    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bb054dbe_8dc5046e",
        "filename": "board/lazor/switchcap.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 110,
      "author": {
        "id": 1451837
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-05-02T23:02:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The intention of ln9310_software_enable(enable) is to enable the switchcap when the enable\u003d1 and disable it when enable\u003d0.\n\nOriginally (i.e. with no software-workaround) the intention was to set the STANDBY_EN register to be 0 permanently and only toggle the nEN pin to start and stop the chip.  Since we need a software-workaround, we could set the nEN to 0 permanently and toggle STANDBY_EN through the software workaround function.  \n\nI was not sure if the toggling of the nEN (GPIO_SWITCHCAP_ON_L) pin was important for some other reason so I decided to leave it in.  There are three scenarios to address:\n\n\t * Initial battery insertion:\n\t * nEN\u003d1 - make sure the part is in. STANDBY mode\n\t * ln9310_init() - initial condition is STANDBY_EN\u003d1\n\t *\n\t * Power up LN9310:\n\t * nEN\u003d0 - STANDBY_EN should be 1 so this doesn\u0027t trigger startup\n\t * ln9310_software_enable(1) - triggers alternate software-based startup\n\t *\n\t * Power down LN9310:\n\t * nEN\u003d1 - shutdown LN9310 (shutdown seq. does not require modification)\n\t * ln9310_software_enable(0) -\treset LN9310 register to state necessary\n\t * \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tfor subsequent startups\n\t */",
      "parentUuid": "84b1e914_64c1a74e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 110,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 110,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "b66d4151e36d467a5a91fa107ad5cbb02f971141",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
